## ABSTRACT

This paper presents a formal explanation for the finite-time blow-up in the three-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations, as recently demonstrated by OpenAI's multi-agent proof system. We argue that the singularity is not a physical prediction but a **mathematical artifact**—a direct consequence of Euler's number \( e \) as the numerical signature of the gap between the ideal and the real.

We demonstrate that the blow-up is the inevitable result of formalizing a continuous, ideal fluid in a system built on the idealizations of classical mathematics. The singularity is the gap made manifest—the point at which the map of the fluid breaks against the territory it cannot capture. This paper establishes that the Navier-Stokes blow-up is not a statement about fluid dynamics but a statement about the **limits of formal representation itself**.

