A critical reader may ask: why \( e \)? Why not \( \pi \), or \( \sqrt{2} \), or any other irrational constant? The answer lies in the **genesis** of \( e \).

\( \pi \) emerges from the geometry of circles—a ratio inherent in the structure of space. \( \sqrt{2} \) emerges from the diagonal of a square—a ratio inherent in the structure of number. Both are **discovered** in the relationships between ideal objects.

\( e \) is different. \( e \) emerges from the **process of approach**—the limit of continuous compounding. It is the constant that defines what it means to approach a value without reaching it. It is the mathematical signature of **process itself**.

When we formalize any continuous process—fluid flow, population growth, radioactive decay—we embed \( e \) into the formalism. It is the constant of the **idealized limit**. Therefore, it is the most direct numerical signature of the gap between the formal and the real.

### 2.2 The Gap Constant and the Third Dimension

The gap constant \( \mathcal{G}_c = e/3 \) is not arbitrary. The factor of \( 1/3 \) arises from the dimensionality of the problem.

The Navier-Stokes equations in three dimensions describe the evolution of a vector field in a three-dimensional space. The number of degrees of freedom per point is three (the three velocity components). The gap—the accumulated formalization error—is distributed across these three degrees of freedom. Therefore, the effective gap constant is \( e/3 \).

